Authorization Model — Scope and Limitations

PURPOSE

This document defines exactly what the effective-permission resolver in
effective_permission.py evaluates, and what it deliberately leaves out. It exists so
that anyone reading a report generated by this tool -- including a future version of
the author -- can tell the difference between "the tool didn't find this" and "AWS
would never have allowed this anyway."

One-line framing: this tool answers "what could this identity plausibly reach under
simplified assumptions," not "what would AWS's policy evaluation engine actually
authorize." It is a reconnaissance and hypothesis-generation aid, not a substitute
for AWS's authorization logic.

WHAT THE RESOLVER EVALUATES

- Direct policy attachments -- a policy attached directly to a user
  (user -> attached-policy -> policy).

- Group membership -- permissions a user inherits by belonging to a group
  (user -> member-of -> group -> attached-policy -> policy).

- Role assumption, followed transitively -- any role an identity is trusted to
  assume (trusted-to-assume), including chains of roles trusting further roles
  (user -> roleA -> roleB -> ... -> policy). Traversal terminates safely even when
  roles trust each other in a cycle.

- Role-attached policies -- policies attached to any role reached this way.

- Allow statements only, expanded across the full action x resource cross-product,
  so one statement with multiple actions and/or resources produces one
  EffectivePermission per action-resource pair.

Every result includes a "via" path: the identity, every intermediate group/role, and
the policy that granted the permission, in order -- giving a single concrete
explanation for why the tool believes the permission exists.

WHAT THE RESOLVER DOES NOT EVALUATE

These are deliberate scope decisions, not oversights. Each represents part of AWS's
real authorization logic that this tool does not implement.

- Deny statements
  Present in the parsed data, but never checked against matching Allow statements.
  A real Deny can override an Allow; this tool will still report the permission as
  granted.

- Condition blocks
  Ignored on both identity policies and trust policies. A permission or trust
  relationship that only applies under a specific condition (source IP, MFA, source
  account, etc.) is treated as unconditional.

- NotAction / NotResource
  Not modeled by the policy parser. Statements using these are effectively skipped
  or mishandled rather than correctly negated.

- Permission boundaries
  Not fetched or applied. A boundary that restricts an identity's maximum
  permissions is invisible to this resolver.

- Service Control Policies (SCPs)
  Not evaluated. An org-level SCP that blocks an action will not be reflected here.

- Session policies
  Not evaluated -- relevant when permissions are further restricted at the moment a
  role is assumed.

- Resource-based policies
  Bucket policies, KMS key policies, and similar resource-side grants/denies are
  not considered. Only identity-side policies are evaluated.

- Account-root trust exception
  A trust policy naming the account root (rather than a specific principal ARN)
  requires the calling identity to also hold sts:AssumeRole permission on that
  role. This resolver treats any trusted-to-assume relationship as sufficient on
  its own, without checking the caller's own policies.

VIA PATH SEMANTICS

"via" records one reachability path per EffectivePermission, not every possible path.
When an identity can reach the same policy through more than one route (for example,
two different roles that both trust a third role), only the first path discovered
during traversal is recorded; later duplicate discoveries of the same node are
skipped rather than recorded again.

This means: absence of a second path in the output does not mean a second path
doesn't exist. Full path enumeration (finding every route between two nodes) is
deferred to the relationship graph built in a later session, which retains every
relationship and is the correct layer for "show me all ways X could reach Y" queries.

SUMMARY

This resolver is a first-order approximation of effective access: direct grants,
group inheritance, and transitive role assumption, restricted to Allow statements.
It is intentionally scoped this way so that its traversal logic (composition of
existing inventory data) stays separate from full AWS policy evaluation, which is a
substantially larger and more failure-prone problem. Any conclusion drawn from this
tool's output should be treated as "worth investigating further," not as a
definitive authorization decision.

